## Lore

When the Great Cataclysm struck, Komkor Bakhunin, one of the old Soviet
Union''s finest tank commanders, happened to be at the Medvedev Complex. Through
the long winter underground he trained the officers who kept the Tank Corps''
standards alive and wrote its manual of rules, drills and doctrine, so that
Russia''s armored experience would survive even while its tanks sat idle.

When the bunker opened and the Sovietski rose, a generation raised on that
romance of armor rolled out across the steppes. Tankers guard their traditions
fiercely, keeping the same tactics, designs and often the same machines for
centuries, and they brook no meddling from recruits, other branches or even
the Director. They fence, wrestle, box and ride for sport, and they fight as
crews so practised that nobody dares change them. In an age of flying cyborgs
and robots, the Sovietski still leans on its tanks and the crews inside them.

## GM Notes

Pick one M.O.S. (crew position) with the skills and the Training of the same
name. Every Tanker knows the basics of every station, command included, so a
crew can swap places in a fight.

On request: any vehicle the Tanker is trained for, aircraft, hover vehicles,
more weapons and E-Clips, a medical kit, a portable computer and language
translator, cameras, surveillance gear, explosives (fusion blocks included),
and low to mid-level military clearance, subject to the commander, supply,
location, casualties and combat conditions.

The save vs Horror Factor rises by +1 at levels 3, 7, 10 and 13.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'sovietski-tanker');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'sovietski-tanker';

